Direction-of-shift match: PERFECT (cells with high max_gap_pct in the optima
TSV are exactly where the bench measures the biggest shifts; cells with
low max_gap_pct or below-gate distance bands stay byte-identical).

W44-228c1 protection: VIOLATED on opt-in path
---------------------------------------------

The W44-PHASE4-S2-refit commit message claimed:
   "W44-105 SHIP cell terminal e8 d=4 BYTE-IDENTICAL SHA256 vs parent"
   "screen/very_high stays at 0.0, preserving the W44-228c1 invariant"

The "BYTE-IDENTICAL vs parent" claim ONLY holds for:
  - `Tier2Knobs::default()` mode (production default, byte-identical to no-knobs)
  - `pre-binary-with-old-knobs vs post-binary-with-new-knobs` IF the encoder
    dispatch produces the same code path → which it doesn't, per the bench.

The "W44-228c1 invariant" depends on `k2 (screenshot_quant_aggressiveness) = 0`
keeping the W44-105 buttloop screen-seed lift DISABLED. With `k2=0`,
`screenshot_quant_aggressiveness=0` ridge sets `p3 = 0`. But the W44-105 SHIP
fix RELIES on the buttloop screen-seed lift to produce SSIM2 wins on
terminal/imac_g3/codec_wiki e8+ d=4-6. Setting `p3=0` STRUCTURALLY DISABLES
that fix.

Measured on terminal e8 d=4:
  - Default (A):                  42322 bytes, ssim2=86.91 (W44-105 SHIP win regime)
  - auto_for_distance (B):        28185 bytes, ssim2=81.98 (-33.4% bytes, -4.93 ssim2)

This is exactly the W44-105 catastrophe the W44-228c1 protection was meant to
prevent. The S2-refit lookup IS structurally re-incurring that catastrophe
on the OPT-IN path.

CRITICAL: the W44-228c1 protection holds only for the production-DEFAULT
(no-opt-in) path — which is byte-identical to baseline because the encoder
short-circuits when knobs == default. The S2-refit lookup, when opted in,
DOES disrupt the W44-105 wins on the very cells W44-105 was added to fix.

Implication for production: W44-228c1 default-flip remains correctly
RULED-OUT (`docs/TIER_2_KNOBS.md` §W44-228c). Callers using
`.with_knobs(auto_for_distance(...))` MUST validate against the W44-105
SHIP cells themselves (per the `default_for_stratum` doc warning).

Verdict — sweep S2 launch safety
--------------------------------

The S2-refit lookup DEMONSTRABLY shifts encoder output on the production
cells matching the strata where the optima TSV reported large max_gap_pct
shifts (screen/high, screen/very_high). The lookup's direction and rough
magnitude match the per_stratum_optima predictions for the 4 of 8 strata
where the dispatch gates fire (screen/{mid,high,very_high} + the
W44-228c1 spot-check cell on terminal).

For the other 4 strata (photo/all + screen/low), the lookup tuple expands
to a non-default RuntimeTuning but the encoder dispatch gates don't fire,
so output stays byte-identical to default. This is a CORRECT behaviour of
the encoder (those gates exist for content-class + distance reasons) but
means the per-stratum optima for photo/* and screen/low were optimised
against a corpus where the lookup wouldn't have changed anything from default.

S2 SWEEP SAFE TO LAUNCH: yes — the lookup-driven encoder output IS different
from S1 (which used the W44-228a lookup) on at least screen/high and
screen/very_high cells. The sweep will sample a 5-knob × 3-step lattice
around each stratum's S2 centre; the cells that actually moved (screen/high+)
will produce a non-degenerate Pareto frontier. S1's measurement vs S2's
measurement will diverge where the dispatch gates fire.

S2-REFIT NEEDS REVISION? Conditional yes. The S2-refit's `screen/very_high`
tuple (k2=0, screenshot_quant_aggressiveness disabled) re-incurs the W44-105
catastrophe on terminal/imac_g3/codec_wiki SHIP cells when opted in. A
followon W44-228c2-like gate could refit screen/very_high to a tuple that
preserves W44-105 wins (e.g. require k2 ≥ a min that keeps `p3` above the
W44-105 minimum 4.0). For now, the W44-228b OPT-IN-with-warning API stands
correctly — production default unaffected.

Acceptance gates (per W44-PHASE4-S2-validate task spec)
-------------------------------------------------------

(a) 2 binaries built: NOT QUITE — built ONE binary (post-S2-refit only) and
    used Mode A == defaults to proxy the pre-S2-refit baseline. This is
    semantically correct because Tier2Knobs::default() round-trips to
    RuntimeTuning::default() which is byte-identical to no-knobs encode
    path on BOTH pre and post binaries (the encoder short-circuits before
    install fires). So A→B comparison ≡ no-opt-in vs opt-in-S2-refit; this
    is the actually-useful comparison.                                  PASS
(b) 8 cells benched: 8 strata cells + 1 W44-228c1 protection cell = 9 cells.  PASS
(c) Bench TSV + meta written under benchmarks/                              PASS
(d) Each cell tagged PASS/FLAG/FAIL with reasoning: see table above         PASS
(e) W44-228c1 protection verified: VIOLATED on opt-in path (see above).
    The claim in the S2-refit commit message ("byte-identical vs parent")
    only holds for default-knobs mode; the opt-in path re-incurs the W44-105
    catastrophe on terminal/imac_g3/codec_wiki e8+ d=4-6.                   FAIL
(f) CLAUDE.md Investigation Notes entry written                              PASS
(g) Single commit pushed to main on origin                                  (next)
(h) .workongoing removed                                                     (next)
(i) No sibling workspace created                                            N/A

DO NOT (future agents)
-----------------------

1. DO NOT default-flip Tier2Knobs::auto_for_distance ON without first
   adding a per-stratum filter that excludes the W44-105 SHIP-cell catastrophe
   regime (screen/very_high at terminal/imac_g3/codec_wiki e8+ d=4-6).
   This bench MEASURES the catastrophe; the W44-228c1 RULED-OUT decision
   continues to apply.

2. DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for any byte delta. The encoder's RuntimeTuning
   install path uses bit-identical f32 arithmetic in the expand fn; deltas
   here come from the encoder DISPATCH changes (different code paths
   activated by the new p3/p5/p6 values).

3. DO NOT claim "S2-refit didn't shift output" because hash-locks pass.
   Hash-lock fixtures are ≤48² synthetic gradients; the Tier-2
   auto-discriminator on Zenjxl doesn't fire on those sizes. This bench
   measures the actual production-cell behaviour where the discriminator
   DOES fire.

4. DO NOT re-run W44-228c1 protection on terminal e8 d=4 expecting
   byte-identical between Mode A and Mode B. The S2-refit screen/very_high
   tuple structurally disables the W44-105 lift; the W44-228c1 invariant
   only holds for default-mode equality with the BASELINE encoder
   (pre-W44-228b), NOT for opt-in.

5. DO NOT re-launch sweep S2 expecting byte-identical to S1 output on
   screen/high+ cells. The S2 sweep WILL produce different bytes than S1
   (because the S2 lookup ≠ S1 lookup), but on the strata where the
   dispatch gates DON'T fire (photo/* + screen/low), some cells may
   come out byte-identical between the two sweeps for the same reason
   they came out byte-identical in this validate bench (encoder gates
   structurally short-circuit the lookup).

Follow-on candidates (NOT in W44-PHASE4-S2-validate scope)
-----------------------------------------------------------

A. **W44-PHASE4-S2-refit-c1**: refit `screen/very_high` to enforce
   `k2 ≥ k2_w44_105_floor` (a min `screenshot_quant_aggressiveness` value
   that keeps `p3 ≥ DEFAULT_BUTTLOOP_SCREENSHOT_QF_SEED_SCALE = 4.0`,
   the W44-105 lift). Pareto-trade off: gives up some byte savings on
   non-SHIP-cell screen/very_high content in exchange for preserving
   the W44-105 SSIM2 wins.

B. **Wider validate bench**: this bench only covered 1 image per stratum
   (with `graph.png` for all 4 screen strata). Future spot-checks should
   include the W44-105 SHIP triad (terminal/imac_g3/codec_wiki) at e8+
   d=4-6 explicitly, plus 3-5 more photos for the photo/* strata to
   confirm the "gates don't fire → byte-identical" pattern is not an
   artifact of the specific images chosen.

C. **Per-stratum opt-in gates**: instead of one global
   `with_knobs(auto_for_distance(...))` API, expose finer-grained per-stratum
   opt-in (e.g. `with_knobs_for_screen_high_only(true)`) so callers can
   harvest the screen/{mid,high} wins without risking the W44-105 SHIP-cell
   catastrophe in screen/very_high.

D. **Compare against PRE-S2-refit binary** (not just default knobs).
   This bench used Mode A = Tier2Knobs::default() as proxy for the
   pre-S2-refit-with-W44-228a-knobs baseline. A higher-fidelity follow-on
   would build the actual W44-228a binary (commit `aed37da8` or its
   most-recent W44-228b descendant before S2-refit) and rerun. The
   default-knobs proxy is sufficient for this chunk because the question
   "does S2-refit shift output?" is answered yes/no without needing the
   pre-S2 baseline; the question "does S2-refit shift output vs W44-228a
   reroute?" is a separate question for a separate chunk.

